---
/**
 * t36 · Shield
 *
 * The same argument, leaning the other way. The shoulders are cut back three
 * pixels and the base five, so the carrier reads as though it is settling on
 * its bottom edge, and the tail hangs on the right rather than the left. The
 * light comes from the upper right to match, which is what separates it from
 * the other three at a glance. The body is symmetric about x=16 — 22 rows,
 * 28 across at its widest — and the tail is exempt from that, as it is in
 * every mark in the family. It is cut from the same silhouette rather than
 * stuck on the side of one, so a single keyline runs round the two of them
 * and there is no seam where they meet.
 *
 * The interior is not a flat fill. Eight surface tones are laid down as a
 * 5-pixel contour bevel that follows every facet: each pixel takes its tone
 * from the local surface normal against a light off (24, 4), and from how
 * far it sits from that light, so the highlight gathers on the facets
 * nearest it and falls away across the rest instead of ringing the whole rim
 * evenly. Facets turned away from the light fall two tones below the body
 * colour. Neighbouring pixels never skip a tone.
 *
 * The middle stays plain. The 8-wide md face with the compact smile sits on
 * the core tone with 7 rows of body air above it and 7 below, measured on
 * the body and ignoring the tail, and nothing is cleared behind it.
 *
 * 9 tones, all purple, and a white face at 6.06:1 on the core.
 */
import MarkFrame from '../MarkFrame.astro';
import { facePathsAt } from '../../../data/mark';

interface Props { size?: number }
const { size = 128 } = Astro.props;
---

